---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import index from '../data/knowledgeIndex.json';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const internal = (segment) => `${base}/${segment.replace(/^\//, '')}`;
const requireObject = (id) => {
  const item = index.objects.find((candidate) => candidate.id === id);
  if (!item) throw new Error(`Publications projection is missing canonical object ${id}`);
  return item;
};

const authorialCapacity = requireObject('object:authorial-capacity');
const epistemicHandoff = requireObject('object:epistemic-handoff');

const sequence = [
  ['01', authorialCapacity.title, 'Exploratory synthesis / working paper', authorialCapacity.status, internal(authorialCapacity.href)],
  ['02', 'Agalmic Economics: The Displaced Scarcity Hypothesis', 'Foundational synthesis / hypothesis', 'Hold for formalization', null],
  ['03', 'Innovation Search Under AI', 'Literature-grounded extension', 'Hold for deeper lineage', null],
  ['04', 'Attention After Candidate Abundance', 'Economics of epistemic abundance', 'Research track', null],
  ['05', 'Markets for Possibility', 'Working paper', 'Research track', null],
  ['06', 'Representation as Discovery', 'Research paper + software', 'Novelty belongs in concrete formalism', null],
  ['07', 'Intellectual Property After Epistemic Abundance', 'Policy / economics paper', 'Research track', null],
];

const drafts = [
  [epistemicHandoff.title, epistemicHandoff.status, internal(epistemicHandoff.href)],
  ['From Machine Discovery to Distributed Discovery: Provenance, Priority and Epistemic Authority in Human–AI Research', 'Paper idea · established problem, operational extension', null],
  ['Credit Without Authority', 'Paper idea · CRediT/guarantorship lineage', null],
  ['Epistemic Uptake Under Cognitive Abundance', 'Paper idea · absorptive-capacity extension', null],
  ['Markets for Expert Attention', 'Paper idea · expert-evaluation lineage', null],
  ['The Human-Scale Epistemic Horizon', 'Deep-future synthesis', null],
];
---
<Layout title="Publications" description="The publication strategy, working papers and lineage-aware draft sequence for Agalmic Research.">
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Publications</p>
      <h1>One research object. Many useful forms. No novelty by typography.</h1>
      <p class="lede">A durable research object can become a paper, essay, model or piece of software. Before any of those forms claim a contribution, the project should establish what the literature already knows and what, if anything, remains to add.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Lineage gate</p>
        <h2>No numbered paper without a prior-art position.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">Each paper must say what it inherits before saying what it contributes.</p>
        <p>The living novelty register classifies candidate contributions as established antecedent, independent rediscovery, synthesis, extension, application, operationalization, terminology candidate, novelty unassessed or, only after substantial search, candidate novelty.</p>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>Finding a close antecedent is a reason to improve the paper, not to hide the citation.</strong></p></div>
        <p><a href={internal('lineage')}>Inspect the Intellectual Lineage & Novelty Register →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Epistemic status</p>
        <h2>Publication is not certification.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">A public artefact can be valuable before its curator can defend every part of it.</p>
        <p>Working Paper 001 now cites close prior work on verification gaps, metacognition and claim accountability, and v0.4 further grounds its machine-discovery discussion in decades of computational-discovery, collective-discovery and scientific-priority scholarship.</p>
        <p><a href={internal(authorialCapacity.href)}>Read {authorialCapacity.status} →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Contribution & authority</p>
        <h2>Use standards before inventing roles.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">CRediT for contribution. W3C PROV for provenance. RO-Crate for packaging.</p>
        <p>Agalmic-specific metadata is reserved for claim-level epistemic authority, handoff, branch state, search boundaries and reversal conditions where mature standards do not adequately capture the research question.</p>
        <p><a href="https://github.com/TsatsuAmable/agalmicresearch/blob/main/docs/STANDARDS_PROVENANCE_PROFILE.md">Read the standards-first provenance profile ↗</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Publication architecture</p>
        <h2>Record first. Search lineage. Refine continuously.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">Working paper → peer-reviewed article → public essay → policy brief → talk → model → code.</p>
        <p class="muted">These are projections of the same evolving research object. Stable versions should retain provenance, contribution roles, lineage status and corrections rather than presenting each release as an immaculate new beginning.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="section-number">Initial sequence</span>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2>The first paper family.</h2>
          <p>Current published objects draw identity and status from the semantic registry. Unpublished rows below remain a roadmap rather than being promoted into canonical research objects before they exist.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="index-list">
        {sequence.map(([n, title, type, status, href]) => (
          <article class="index-item">
            <span class="index-meta">{n}</span>
            <div>
              <h3>{href ? <a href={href}>{title}</a> : title}</h3>
              <p class="muted">{type}</p>
            </div>
            <span class="index-status">{status}</span>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="section-number">Draft notebook</span>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2>Research directions before sequence.</h2>
          <p>Only actual draft objects are linked from the semantic registry. The remaining rows are deliberately kept as future research directions rather than pretending every possibility has already become a document.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="index-list">
        {drafts.map(([title, status, href], i) => (
          <article class="index-item">
            <span class="index-meta">D{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <div>
              <h3>{href ? <a href={href}>{title}</a> : title}</h3>
              <p class="muted">Conceptual development / future research</p>
            </div>
            <span class="index-status">{status}</span>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button secondary" href={internal('drafts')}>Browse all drafts & ideas</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Distribution</p>
        <h2>Make the work easy to find, cite, contest and correct.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Website:</strong> canonical index, lineage, revisions, epistemic status and links between research threads.</li>
          <li><strong>GitHub:</strong> source, models, data, standards-compatible provenance and version history.</li>
          <li><strong>RO-Crate:</strong> package research objects with interoperable metadata where feasible.</li>
          <li><strong>Research archives:</strong> stable snapshots through Zenodo, OSF or SSRN where suitable.</li>
          <li><strong>Peer review:</strong> domain scrutiny selected paper by paper, especially where the curator lacks authority.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
